<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankfurt Judengasse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Judengasse was the walled Jewish quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main, to which the city confined its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from 1462. Its single narrow street became one of the most crowded places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and it was the birthplace of the Rothschild banking family. Its confinement ended after a French bombardment destroyed much of it in 1796, and remains excavated in the 1980s are now shown in a museum.</p></div>
